    cgroup version   : 2  (v2 is required for reliable memory accounting)

--- applied limits, read back from the daemon ---
    CONTAINER             MEM_LIMIT     MEM_RESV      CPU_LIMIT      MEMSWAP
    postgres                  512 M        256 M         1.00 cpu       1024 M
    backend                   256 M         64 M         0.75 cpu        256 M
    nginx                     128 M         32 M         0.50 cpu        256 M

    A non-zero value in every column is the proof that the compose
    deploy.resources block is being enforced, not silently ignored.

    Read the MEMSWAP column carefully. Where it is DOUBLE the memory limit
    (postgres 512->1024, nginx 128->256) that is Docker's default: a
    container may use its limit again in swap, so memory pressure degrades
    into paging rather than an OOM kill. Only the backend sets
    memswap_limit equal to its memory limit, which disables swap entirely.
    That is deliberate: without it the RAM-exhaustion drill could page
    instead of dying and would never produce OOMKilled: true.

